@article{Munro1993,
	abstract = {Productions of 10 English vowels in /bVt/ & /bVd/ contexts were elicited
	from a group of native American English speakers & a group of native
	Arabic speakers who had learned English in adulthood (N = 23 each,
	aged 19-57). When a variety of acoustic measurements were examined,
	the two groups differed on at least one of these parameters for nearly
	every vowel considered. A subset of the Arabic speakers' productions,
	& the productions of two native English speakers, were rated for
	accentedness by five native English-speaking judges, who indicated
	that only a minority of the Arabic group's productions were "native-like."
	When the acoustic measurement data were regressed on the mean ratings,
	it was found that the accentedness scores were correlated primarily
	with F1 frequency & movement in F2, although the significant predictors
	varied from vowel to vowel. 5 Tables, 3 Figures, 46 References. Adapted
